import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Victim Family Outreach & Advocacy | Silver Lining of Hope"
  description="Free, personal advocacy for families of homicide victims and missing persons, and for domestic violence survivors, across Indiana and nearby states."
  heading="Outreach & advocacy"
  intro="Research moves cases. Advocacy carries people. This pillar is the human side of everything we do."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Programs', href: '/programs' },
    { label: 'Outreach & advocacy', href: '/programs/outreach-advocacy' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="container--narrow section prose">
    <p>
      The "O" in HOPE stands for outreach and advocacy — the work of standing
      next to a family while everything we research plays out. A case file
      doesn't return phone calls. It doesn't sit with you at a hearing, or
      explain what a prosecutor's letter means, or push back when a family is
      told, again, to "be patient." People do that. This pillar is how we do
      it.
    </p>

    <h2>What advocacy looks like in practice</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A single point of contact.</strong> Families working with us
        get one consistent advocate who knows their case, so they never have
        to re-tell the worst story of their lives to a stranger.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Navigating agencies.</strong> We help families understand who
        actually owns their case — police department, sheriff, state police,
        coroner, prosecutor — what each one can do, and how to communicate
        with them effectively and respectfully.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Meetings and hearings.</strong> When a family wants us there,
        we attend case meetings, court dates, and parole or sentencing
        hearings with them — prepared, quiet, and on their side.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Media guidance.</strong> Press attention can help a case or
        hurt it. We help families decide when to speak, prepare statements,
        and deal with reporters on their own terms — never ours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Support for domestic violence survivors.</strong> Many hidden
        homicides begin as domestic violence. We help survivors safety-plan,
        document abuse, and connect with hotlines, shelters, and legal
        resources — see our <a href="/get-help/resources">resources page</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Trauma-informed, always</h2>
    <p>
      The people we serve are grieving, traumatized, or in danger — sometimes
      all three. We work at each family's pace, ask before we act, and never
      publish anything about a case without the family's consent. Survivors
      browsing this site can use the <strong>Quick exit</strong> button at any
      time, and our <a href="/get-help/submit-a-tip">tip form</a> collects
      nothing that identifies you unless you choose to share it.
    </p>

    <h2>Beyond individual families</h2>
    <p>
      Outreach also means showing up for the wider community of the missing
      and murdered: organizing awareness events for unsolved cases, supporting
      vigils and remembrance gatherings, amplifying verified information about
      active missing person searches, and connecting families with each other
      — because no one understands this road like someone walking it too.
      Find upcoming gatherings on our <a href="/get-involved/events">events
      page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Ask us to walk with you</h2>
    <p>
      If your family needs an advocate, start at
      <a href="/get-help/families">Get help for families</a> or
      <a href="/about/contact">contact us</a> directly. If you want to be
      part of this work, we train volunteers for outreach roles —
      <a href="/get-involved/volunteer">learn about volunteering</a>. Like
      everything at Silver Lining of Hope, advocacy is free, and it is
      offered in the name of Leslie "Hope" Houston —
      <a href="/about/our-story">the reason we exist</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
